{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.474da30668","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-373","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Cresthill is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1932.</li><li>Its surface area is 560 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Larkspur is in southern New Zealand. It was first surveyed in 1976.</li><li>Its surface area is 389 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the surface area of the two lakes differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Cresthill was first surveyed in 1932, while Lake Larkspur was first surveyed in 1976.","Both Lake Cresthill and Lake Larkspur are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","Lake Cresthill, which is in southern Norway, is one of several freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","The surface area of Lake Cresthill (560 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Larkspur (389 square kilometers)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the surface area of both lakes. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one lake."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.472a0a4377","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-377","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Glenrock Observatory is in northern Chile. It began operating in 1909.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 11 meters.</li><li>The Eastwick Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1963.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 2 meters.</li><li>Both are observatories located on remote mountaintops.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two observatorys began operating.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Glenrock Observatory and the Eastwick Observatory are observatories located on remote mountaintops.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Glenrock Observatory (11 meters) is greater than that of the Eastwick Observatory (2 meters).","The Glenrock Observatory is in northern Chile, and the Eastwick Observatory is in southern New Zealand.","The Glenrock Observatory began operating in 1909, while the Eastwick Observatory began operating in 1963."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a3463e326c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-382","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Alder Tower is in southern New Zealand. It was completed in 1890.</li><li>Its height is 388 meters.</li><li>The Wexford Tower is in western Scotland. It was completed in 1975.</li><li>Its height is 175 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two towers differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of the Alder Tower (388 meters) is greater than that of the Wexford Tower (175 meters).","The Alder Tower was completed in 1890, while the Wexford Tower was completed in 1975.","Both the Alder Tower and the Wexford Tower are observation towers open to the public.","The Alder Tower, which is in southern New Zealand, is one of several observation towers open to the public."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both towers. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tower."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9c18c7960e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-387","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Alder Museum is in eastern Australia. It opened in 1919.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 652,421 visitors.</li><li>The Quarry Museum is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 1964.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 602,601 visitors.</li><li>Both are art museums with free admission.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the annual attendance of the two museums differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Alder Museum opened in 1919, while the Quarry Museum opened in 1964.","The Alder Museum, which is in eastern Australia, is one of several art museums with free admission.","Both the Alder Museum and the Quarry Museum are art museums with free admission.","The annual attendance of the Alder Museum (652,421 visitors) is greater than that of the Quarry Museum (602,601 visitors)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the annual attendance of both museums. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one museum."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3d7ed72f0f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-388","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Umber Tunnel is in central Canada. It opened in 2015.</li><li>Its length is 21 kilometers.</li><li>The Ironwood Tunnel is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 1894.</li><li>Its length is 17 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the length of the two tunnels differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Umber Tunnel and the Ironwood Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The Umber Tunnel, which is in central Canada, is one of several tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The Umber Tunnel opened in 2015, while the Ironwood Tunnel opened in 1894.","The length of the Umber Tunnel (21 kilometers) is greater than that of the Ironwood Tunnel (17 kilometers)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the length of both tunnels. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tunnel."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c1f3f07438","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-390","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Valewood Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1999.</li><li>Its length is 11 kilometers.</li><li>The Eastwick Tunnel is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 2011.</li><li>Its length is 5 kilometers.</li><li>Both are railway tunnels.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Valewood Tunnel and the Eastwick Tunnel.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The length of the Valewood Tunnel (11 kilometers) is greater than that of the Eastwick Tunnel (5 kilometers).","The Valewood Tunnel is in southern Norway, and the Eastwick Tunnel is in southern New Zealand.","The Valewood Tunnel opened in 1999, while the Eastwick Tunnel opened in 2011.","Both the Valewood Tunnel and the Eastwick Tunnel are railway tunnels."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7a06d16079","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-391","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Quarry Observatory is in northern Chile. It began operating in 2015.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>The Sable Observatory is in western Kenya. It began operating in 1955.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 6 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two observatorys began operating.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Quarry Observatory and the Sable Observatory are radio observatories.","The Quarry Observatory is in northern Chile, and the Sable Observatory is in western Kenya.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Quarry Observatory (8 meters) is greater than that of the Sable Observatory (6 meters).","The Quarry Observatory began operating in 2015, while the Sable Observatory began operating in 1955."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4ff34dbbf6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-391","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Quarry Observatory is in northern Chile. It began operating in 2015.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>The Sable Observatory is in western Kenya. It began operating in 1955.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 6 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two observatorys.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Quarry Observatory, which is in northern Chile, is one of several radio observatories.","The Quarry Observatory began operating in 2015, while the Sable Observatory began operating in 1955.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Quarry Observatory (8 meters) is greater than that of the Sable Observatory (6 meters).","The Quarry Observatory is in northern Chile, and the Sable Observatory is in western Kenya."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.041327401b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-398","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Glenrock Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1913.</li><li>Its length is 22 kilometers.</li><li>The Brookhaven Tunnel is in southern Brazil. It opened in 1974.</li><li>Its length is 14 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Glenrock Tunnel and the Brookhaven Tunnel.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Glenrock Tunnel opened in 1913, while the Brookhaven Tunnel opened in 1974.","The Glenrock Tunnel is in southern Norway, and the Brookhaven Tunnel is in southern Brazil.","Both the Glenrock Tunnel and the Brookhaven Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The length of the Glenrock Tunnel (22 kilometers) is greater than that of the Brookhaven Tunnel (14 kilometers)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.951edc99d6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-34","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Takeda is an entomologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Takeda set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Takeda found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Takeda concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.","Takeda concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.","Entomologist Tomás Takeda set out to learn whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.","To investigate the question, Takeda set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4d5e321e56","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-64","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Imani Lindqvist is an astronomer.</li><li>Lindqvist wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Lindqvist measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Lindqvist found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Lindqvist concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Lindqvist measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Lindqvist concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","Because the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days, Lindqvist concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","Lindqvist found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b81699d919","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-69","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Varga is a linguist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Varga gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Varga found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Varga concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.","Varga concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Linguist Felipe Varga set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.","To investigate the question, Varga gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.00b62b1b29","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-74","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Moreau is an economist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Moreau tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Moreau found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Moreau concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Moreau concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","To investigate the question, Moreau tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.","Economist Felipe Moreau set out to learn whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.","Moreau found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ae3b4bb555","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-77","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Nadia Duarte is an economist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Duarte tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Duarte found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Duarte concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Economist Nadia Duarte set out to learn whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.","Duarte concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","Duarte found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.","To investigate the question, Duarte tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0870cb63dd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-82","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Anika Brennan is a botanist.</li><li>Brennan wanted to find out whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.</li><li>Brennan grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.</li><li>Brennan found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.</li><li>Brennan concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Brennan found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.","To investigate the question, Brennan grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.","Brennan concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.","Botanist Anika Brennan set out to learn whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3b1aa95119","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-96","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Hana Brennan is an education researcher.</li><li>Brennan wanted to find out whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.</li><li>Brennan had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.</li><li>Brennan found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.</li><li>Brennan concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Brennan found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.","To investigate the question, Brennan had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.","Brennan concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.","Education researcher Hana Brennan set out to learn whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.440d375abe","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-98","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Mensah is an education researcher.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.</li><li>Mensah had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.</li><li>Mensah found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.</li><li>Mensah concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Mensah had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.","Mensah concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.","Because children in the read-aloud group learned more new words, Mensah concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.","Mensah found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.47cdf944ed","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-103","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Delacroix is a chemist.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Delacroix placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Delacroix found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Delacroix placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.","Delacroix concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.","Delacroix found that most samples broke apart within four months.","Chemist Chen Delacroix set out to learn whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c63b9546d7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-110","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Mensah is a primatologist.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Mensah observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Mensah found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Mensah concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier, Mensah concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.","Mensah found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","To investigate the question, Mensah observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.","Mensah concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1cbc626e79","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-126","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Delacroix is a public health researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Delacroix counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Delacroix found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Delacroix found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.","To investigate the question, Delacroix counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.","Because cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year, Delacroix concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.","Delacroix concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0f0274ac18","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-130","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dmitri Iyer is a public health researcher.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Iyer counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Iyer found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Iyer concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Public health researcher Dmitri Iyer set out to learn whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.","To investigate the question, Iyer counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.","Iyer found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.","Iyer concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.364bdfbc6e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-136","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Mbeki is an archaeologist.</li><li>Mbeki wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Mbeki analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Mbeki found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Mbeki concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mbeki found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","Because many pots were made from clay found only far inland, Mbeki concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","To investigate the question, Mbeki analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Mbeki concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2077767e5f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-145","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Takeda is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Takeda fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Takeda found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Takeda concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","Marine ecologist Priya Takeda set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","To investigate the question, Takeda fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Takeda concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7b26a156d1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-161","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Jonas Solberg is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Solberg wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Solberg moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Solberg found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Solberg concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Solberg concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.","Behavioral economist Jonas Solberg set out to learn whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.","Solberg found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","To investigate the question, Solberg moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8378fbc4fa","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-163","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Jonas Lindqvist is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Lindqvist wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Lindqvist played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Lindqvist found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Lindqvist concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Lindqvist played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.","Veterinary scientist Jonas Lindqvist set out to learn whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.","Lindqvist found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","Lindqvist concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4debf5df39","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-178","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Mensah is a music researcher.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Mensah tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Mensah found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Mensah concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mensah found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.","Music researcher Ingrid Mensah set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.","Mensah concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","To investigate the question, Mensah tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."}
